# Sanity Content Architecture

## Source of truth

Sanity owns content that the client may need to edit without code changes.

Recommended managed content:
- Products
- Product categories
- Journal/recipes
- Testimonials
- Site settings
- Navigation settings if required
- Homepage featured content
- SEO fields for managed documents

## Product schema

A product should support, as appropriate:
- name
- slug
- category
- shortDescription
- description
- images
- gallery ordering
- availability
- seasonal
- growingMethod
- badges/labels
- suitableFor
- packSizes
- culinaryUses
- storageAndHandling
- relatedProducts
- featured
- SEO title
- SEO description
- SEO image

Keep fields meaningful and avoid schema bloat.

## Schema conventions

Use:
- clear titles
- useful descriptions
- validation
- sensible initial values
- ordering that matches the editor's workflow

Required fields should be genuinely required.
Do not make optional editorial fields mandatory just because the front end can display them.

## GROQ

Keep GROQ queries explicit and small.
Do not overfetch every field for every component.

Prefer reusable query fragments or centralized query helpers where that improves consistency.

Do not fetch secrets or private fields into public page data.

## Content relationships

Use Sanity references when editorial relationships matter, such as:
- related products
- featured products
- journal ingredients/products
- testimonials

Avoid duplicating the same content across multiple documents unless there is a clear reason.

## Slugs

Slugs must be:
- lowercase
- stable
- readable
- unique

Never generate URLs from mutable display labels at runtime.

## Missing content

The frontend must tolerate missing optional content.
If a product has no storage section, omit that section cleanly.
Do not render empty headings.

## Preview/editing

Where practical, structure content so editors can understand what appears on the frontend.
Avoid opaque field names.
